## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to reason about memory cgroup enforcement, scalable reclaim under mixed workloads, and kernel-level resource management.

## Key Concepts
- Per-container quotas and deficit calculation
- Per-page state tracking (active, inactive, referenced)
- NUMA-aware page replacement decisions
- Interaction with swap and lazy reclaim mechanisms
- Concurrency-safe quota accounting
- Starvation prevention and thrashing avoidance

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal C-like skeleton of reclaim loop
for (each node in nodes) {
    while (deficit[node] > 0) {
        // Select overcommitted cgroup as victim
        struct cgroup *victim = pick_victim_cgroup(node);

        // Evict pages from victim (prefer inactive, anon > file)
        struct page *page = lru_scan(victim, node);
        if (page) {
            if (try_to_reclaim(page))
                deficit[node] -= PAGE_SIZE;
        }
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical scheduling control, race-free updates, and wakeup semantics; probes fairness under contention.

## Key Concepts
- Per-thread atomic credit accounting
- Per-core run queues and locality
- Blocking/wakeups with per-core wait queues
- Cost accounting and fairness guarantees

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simplified sketch (C-like, using JS-style syntax for clarity)
typedef struct { atomic_int credits; int core; waitqueue_t wq; } thread_t;

void on_tick(int core){
  for (each t in core_list[core]){
    int c = atomic_load(&t->credits);
    if (c < MAX) atomic_fetch_add(&t->credits, 1);
  }
  wake_one_from_wq(core);
}

int spend_credit(thread_t* t, int cost){
  int prev = atomic_fetch_sub(&t->credits, cost);
  return prev - cost;
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle dynamic core migration of threads while preserving correctness?
- How would you adjust MAX to prevent starvation without starving CPU-bound threads?

